+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+The supplied repository was a static page with no installed-app manifest or persisted configuration. It now includes a stable app identity and a small, explicitly scoped preferences store. Actual deployment domains and hosting credentials were not supplied, so this change prepares two deployable bundles; it does not change a live installation.
+
+Install dependencies with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. Build with your actual, same-site HTTPS origins:
+
+```sh
+pnpm build --old-origin=https://coffee.example.com --new-origin=https://app.example.com
+pnpm test
+```
+
+The example hosts are placeholders. The build rejects missing origins, credentials, paths, HTTP, identical origins, unrelated registrable domains, and separate tenants of private suffixes such as github.io. Public Suffix List validation uses `tldts`.
+
+Deploy `dist/new/` to the root of the new origin, then `dist/old/` to the root of the old origin. Include the hidden `.well-known/` directory. Serve `manifest.webmanifest` as `application/manifest+json`, the extensionless association file as `application/json`, and JavaScript as JavaScript. Do not route these files to an HTML fallback or cache the old manifest/association indefinitely. Keep both origins online throughout the rollout.
+
+For an existing installation, preserve its exact old manifest identity. The default assumes its resolved ID (or previous start URL when no explicit ID existed) was `/`. Override both as appropriate:
+
+```sh
+pnpm build --old-origin=https://coffee.example.com --new-origin=https://app.example.com --old-id='/index.html?installed=1' --old-start-url='/index.html?installed=1'
+```
+
+Retain the existing app's name, icons and other manifest integrations if deploying this into an already installed product. This repository had none. Hosts using a non-root old start URL must continue to serve the app at that URL. Both bundles expect root hosting; deploying beneath a path needs a separate asset/scope configuration.
+
+The new manifest declares `migrate_from` with the resolved old app ID. The old manifest's `migrate_to` points to the new app, and its `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` authorizes the full destination app ID. The browser can then update the installation and OS integration after user approval. This follows the [current Chrome migration documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration), including its object-shaped `migrate_to`. The local guidance's array example and Chrome 149 support statement are outdated; Chrome documents this feature starting in version 150.
+
+User configuration is separate from installation migration. At the new address, “Import my settings” opens a small window on the old origin. Both sides check the exact origin, popup identity, protocol message type and a fresh nonce. Only the validated `daily-grind:preferences` schema (appearance and favorite drink) is copied. The original remains untouched. Missing old preferences do not overwrite settings at the destination; an explicit import otherwise replaces destination preferences. Errors, blocked popups, closed windows, and timeouts offer export/restore. A single storage write prevents partially applied configuration.
+
+Keep `/transfer.html`, its modules, the old install page, manifest and association available without redirects. A `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` header can sever the popup connection; use a compatible policy for these pages or use the file backup. Export/restore also handles storage partitioning or unavailable old-origin storage. Transfer backups before retiring the old origin.
+
+Automatic installation migration has limited browser support. The page provides a new-address link and instructions to transfer preferences and manually install when no browser update is offered. Safari/Firefox and other unsupported browsers cannot preserve OS shortcuts automatically. Cookies, sign-in sessions, arbitrary localStorage/IndexedDB data and browser permissions are not copied by this implementation. Add further app-specific configuration to the strict schema if needed; never copy credentials or all storage indiscriminately.
+
+`pnpm test` runs Node checks for manifest generation, same-site validation, preference validation, storage failures and origin/source message checks. No browser, dev/preview server or server entrypoint is used.
